  "readme": "VFX-JS: Visual Effects Framework for Web VFX-JS is a JavaScript library to add WebGL-powered effects to your website. You can easily attach it to normal <img>, <video> elements etc. See also @vfx-js/react for React bindings. Usage Install via npm: npm i @vfx-js/core Then create VFX object in your script: import { VFX } from '@vfx-js/core'; const img = document.querySelector('#img'); const vfx = new VFX(); vfx.add(img, { shader: \"glitch\", overflow: 100 }); Or compose prebuilt effects from @vfx-js/effects: import { VFX } from '@vfx-js/core'; import { BloomEffect, PixelateEffect } from '@vfx-js/effects'; const vfx = new VFX(); vfx.add(img, { effect: [new PixelateEffect({ size: 10 }), new BloomEffect({ intensity: 5 })], }); Examples You can find basic examples on the VFX-JS website. Or visit VFX-JS Examples for more advanced use cases. https://amagi.dev/vfx-js/ https://amagi.dev/vfx-js/examples Author AMAGI LICENSE MIT",
